Protected QG-34 run is GREEN end-to-end on its first execution. Exact adaptive minimax result on the frozen 715-orbit / 92 joint-summary-class observation problem:

- worst-case adaptive depth `D_* = 3`;
- class-depth histogram: `{0:7, 1:30, 2:39, 3:16}`;
- orbit-mass histogram: `{0:7, 1:72, 2:282, 3:354}`;
- 16 worst classes, including both size-48 classes;
- production exact subset-mask DP made 1,065 memoized feasibility calls; independent generic ORION used global-orbit tuple states and reproduced every one of the 92 depths exactly;
- the serialized first worst-class policy has depth 3 and 12 singleton leaves; policy tamper was rejected; native/replay/authority gates passed.

This earns exact adaptive minimax authority for the frozen post-summary identification query. It does **not** yet earn a strict adaptivity advantage: QG-32c's exact minimum 5 is for one universal fixed set, whereas QG-34 conditions on the known summary class. QG-35 #932 was frozen before this QG-34 outcome to compute the fair summary-conditioned nonadaptive baseline `F_*`.

Sealed files: `research/extensions/orion-qg/QG34_ADAPTIVE_PROBE_TREE_RESULTS.json` and `development/orion-qg-regime-geometry/QG34_PROTECTED_RUN_RECEIPT_2026-08-22.json`. Stronger hardware/full-finite/global-state/novelty/physical-advantage claims remain false.
